  "definition_solutions": [
    {
      "blocks": [0],
      "solution": "Work over real x with a > 0 and a != 1. Both restrictions are necessary for the stated real logarithm definition and unique-root conclusion."
    },
    {
      "blocks": [1],
      "solution": "a^x is strictly positive for every real x; hence a^x = b has no real root for b <= 0, including b = 0."
    },
    {
      "blocks": [2],
      "solution": "The exponential has domain R and range (0, +infinity). It is strictly increasing for a > 1 and strictly decreasing for 0 < a < 1. Thus every b > 0 is attained at exactly one x."
    },
    {
      "blocks": [3],
      "solution": "For fig-83's a > 1, a horizontal line at any b > 0 meets the exponential once, while lines at b = 0 and b < 0 do not. The intersection's x-coordinate is log_a b. Its sign is positive for b > 1, zero for b = 1, and negative for 0 < b < 1; the illustrated positive-line intersection lies to the right of the origin and therefore depicts b > 1, not every positive b."
    },
    {
      "blocks": [5, 8, 9],
      "solution": "log_a b is the exponent x, not the base or the power value: log_a b = x if and only if a^x = b, for a > 0, a != 1 and b > 0."
    },
    {
      "blocks": [11, 12],
      "solution": "log_3(-27) and log_2(0) have no real meaning. Generally the logarithm's argument, after any argument-internal arithmetic, must be strictly positive. The real logarithmic function has domain (0, +infinity) and range R. A negative result or a minus sign outside a logarithm does not make a positive argument invalid."
    }
  ],
